# proc_agnos.cyr — AGNOS-native process table, on `proclist` #99 (v0.9.8).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# Until now `shu` on AGNOS rendered the process table's column header and ZERO
# ROWS, and both roadmap.md and state.md recorded that as blocked upstream with
# "no chakshu-side workaround": AGNOS has no procfs, and the ring-3 surface was
# getpid/spawn/waitpid/kill with nothing that could answer "what is running?".
#
# That stopped being true at **agnos 1.56.47**, which minted `#99 proclist`, and
# the cyrius wrapper has shipped since **6.5.35** — i.e. it was already available
# under the pin chakshu had *before* the v0.9.6 bump. The blocker had simply
# outlived its own fix, unnoticed, because nothing re-read the syscall table.
#
#   sys_proclist(buf, max) -> record count, or -1
#
# Record is 64 B, little-endian, one per live slot:
#   +0   u64 pid
#   +8   u64 state     1 = ready · 2 = running · 3 = claiming (0 = dead, skipped)
#   +16  u64 ppid      0 = init
#   +24  u8  name[32]  NUL-terminated basename, recorded by the ELF loader
#   +56  u64 reserved  always 0 today
#
# ⭐ v0.9.9 — CPU% AND MEM% ARE REAL NOW. agnos 1.56.59 filled `+56` in response to
# chakshu's telemetry filing: it is ONE store64 of `(rss_pages << 32) | cpu_ticks`
# (kernel/core/syscall.cyr:10424), so little-endian x86-64 puts
#   load32(rec + 56) = cpu ticks   (100 Hz, cumulative since the SLOT was allocated)
#   load32(rec + 60) = rss pages   (4 KiB units)
# Cyrius `load32` emits a 32-bit `mov`, which zero-extends, so neither half can come
# back negative. ⛔ Do NOT render `load64(rec + 56)` as either field: on a live
# process the packed value is ~6.6e12, which is positive, formats fine, and is
# nonsense as both a tick count and a page count.
#
# ⭐ cyrius 6.6.0 fixed the stale wrapper doc AND shipped the two accessors chakshu
# filed for — `proclist_cpu_ticks(rec)` and `proclist_rss_pages(rec)`. They are used
# below in preference to hand-decoding, which is what their own comment asks for:
# "both halves are plausible positive integers, so a swap produces a wrong number
# rather than an obvious failure".
#
# USER is different and must NOT be lumped in: agnos is single-user, its getuid is
# a literal `return 0` and no per-task uid exists in the kernel at all, so every
# process really is root. That is a known value, not a missing one — it renders
# `root`.
#
# ⚠ MEM% IS 2 MB-GRANULAR, AND THAT IS NOT THE UNIT SUGGESTING OTHERWISE. The page
# count is genuine 4 KiB pages, but the kernel COMPUTES it by walking the process's
# page directory and counting PDEs that are present AND user, adding 512 pages per
# hit (kernel/core/proc.cyr:1455) — agnos's own gate asserts the result is always a
# multiple of 512. So a 22 KB binary reports 3 PDEs = 6 MB. Each counted PDE is a
# real 2 MB physical commitment, so the figure is honest as "physical memory
# committed to this process" — it is NOT Linux-comparable RSS and is not labelled
# as such anywhere user-facing.
#
# ⭐ v0.10.0 — CPU% SHIPS, because agnos fixed the reason it could not.
#
# v0.9.9 built this column, measured chakshu rendering ITSELF at 100% while it slept
# through its own sampling window, and backed it out: the tick charge credited
# `proc_current_get()` unconditionally, so a process halted in a blocking syscall
# accrued at wall-clock rate. Filed upstream; agnos 1.56.60 fixed it and credits the
# measurement by name. `cpu_in_halt[cpu]` is set around the `hlt` in `arch_wait()`
# (kernel/arch/x86_64/io.cyr:181-187) and the timer ISR consumes it read-then-clear
# before deciding whether to charge (kernel/arch/x86_64/pic.cyr:88-99). The tick is
# DROPPED, not re-charged — halted time belongs to nobody — so the numerator is now
# utime+stime, which is exactly what the shared column head means on Linux.
#
# NUMERATOR   delta of `proclist_cpu_ticks(rec)` — halt-excluded 100 Hz ticks.
# DENOMINATOR pooled delta of `sysinfo`#35's +40 band, (user+kern) summed over the
#             online cores. ⛔ NOT wall time: `uptime_ms`#40 is BSP-only and frozen
#             whenever IF is clear, so it would divide a live numerator by a stopped
#             clock.
#
# ⭐ AND THE BAND IS THE RIGHT DENOMINATOR *BECAUSE* IT IS NOT HALT-EXCLUDED. The
# per-core user/kern counters are charged unconditionally, so a halted tick lands in
# `kern`. That asymmetry is deliberate: it makes the pooled sum a true wall-clock
# tick count. The same asymmetry is why `user/(user+kern)` must NEVER be rendered as
# machine-busy — on an idle box `kern` is mostly halt — so chakshu still shows no
# aggregate CPU bar on agnos.
#
# ⚠ NAMELESS ROWS GET n/a, AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC. The halt exclusion covers
# `arch_wait()`, but an AP's idle park is a RAW `hlt` outside it (agnos
# kernel/arch/x86_64/smp.cyr:467) with its LAPIC timer armed — so on a multi-core box
# every idle tick is charged to that core's idle kthread. Names are set only by the
# ELF loader, so every kernel thread has an empty name: suppressing CPU% for nameless
# rows is what stops a wholly idle 4-core machine rendering three phantom rows at
# ~33% each. Invisible on the single-vCPU QEMU harness; wrong on real iron. Filed as
# agnos docs/development/issues/2026-09-07-ap-idle-park-bypasses-halt-guard.md.
#
# ⚠ THE COLUMN DEPENDS ON agnoshi's LAUNCH ROUTING, not on agnos. `run /bin/shu` goes
# through `spawn_path`#43 and gets IF=1 as an ordinary scheduled process. Under
# `execwait`#37 the child runs IF=0, takes no timer interrupt at all, and its tick
# counter reads a flat zero. If chakshu ever measures its own row at 0 while other
# rows move, suspect a #37 revert in agnoshi before suspecting the kernel.
#
# Anything without data is rendered `n/a`, NOT 0 — the rule src/tui.cyr already
# argues for: "a blank field reads as a render bug; `n/a` is the honest answer".
# A row claiming 0% CPU for a spinning process would be a lie the monitor invented.
#
# ⚠ A SNAPSHOT, NOT A HANDLE. The kernel walks its table under preempt_disable,
# so the set cannot change mid-walk, but a pid may exit before we act on it. That
# race belongs to the caller — the kill path re-probes before signalling.
#
# ⚠ GUARD THE CALL. A kernel older than 1.56.47 has no `#99` arm. The ABI note is
# explicit that a negative return means "this kernel cannot enumerate" and must
# NOT be conflated with "the system has no processes" — they are different facts
# and a monitor that confuses them is lying about the machine. `_procs_fill_agnos`
# returns -1 on a negative proclist and leaves `_proc_n` at 0; callers surface
# that as the same degraded state as any other unreadable source.

# The entire body is AGNOS-only: `sys_proclist` is an agnos syscall and does not
# resolve on Linux, so an ungated body would warn on every lean Linux build.
# Same posture as src/tui_agnos.cyr (audit AG-7).
#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS

var PROCLIST_REC_SIZE = 64;
var PROCLIST_ST_READY = 1;
var PROCLIST_ST_RUN   = 2;
var PROCLIST_ST_CLAIM = 3;

# Sentinel for "the kernel does not track this". Distinct from 0, which is a
# legitimate measured value for cpu% and rss on Linux.
var PROC_NA = 0 - 1;

# Lazy scratch for the proclist snapshot: PROC_MAX * 64 B, alloc'd once and
# reused every frame. Module-scope rather than a local because PROC_MAX * 64 is
# 512 KB, far past any per-fn stack budget.
var _proclist_buf = 0;

fn _proclist_ensure() {
    if (_proclist_buf == 0) {
        _proclist_buf = alloc(PROC_MAX * PROCLIST_REC_SIZE);
    }
    return 0;
}

# Map an agnos scheduler state to the single status character the table renders.
#
# Linux's `R` covers TASK_RUNNING, which is BOTH on-CPU and on-runqueue, so
# folding agnos's `ready` and `running` into `R` is not a loss of fidelity —
# it is exactly the Linux semantics a reader already knows. `claiming` has no
# Linux counterpart and gets its own letter rather than being forced into one.
fn _proclist_state_char(st) {
    if (st == PROCLIST_ST_READY) { return 82; }     # 'R' — runnable
    if (st == PROCLIST_ST_RUN)   { return 82; }     # 'R' — on-CPU
    if (st == PROCLIST_ST_CLAIM) { return 67; }     # 'C' — claiming
    return 63;                                       # '?' — unknown to us
}

# Pooled CPU ticks across every online core, or -1 if the band is unreadable.
# This is the CPU% DENOMINATOR — see the header for why wall time is not.
fn _agnos_tick_pool() {
    var si[208];
    if (sys_sysinfo_n(&si, SYSINFO_SIZE_CPU) < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    # cpu_count is the ONLINE core count; the band has 4 fixed slots. Clamp both
    # ways so a partial AP check-in cannot index past the band.
    var ncpu = load64(&si + SI_CPUS);
    if (ncpu < 1) { ncpu = 1; }
    if (ncpu > 4) { ncpu = 4; }
    var total = 0;
    var c = 0;
    while (c < ncpu) {
        total = total + sysinfo_cpu_user(&si, c) + sysinfo_cpu_kern(&si, c);
        c = c + 1;
    }
    return total;
}

# Previous frame's pooled tick total, and whether one has been taken.
var _agnos_pool_prev  = 0;
var _agnos_pool_valid = 0;

# Seed the tick baseline: per-pid ticks into _proc_map, plus the pooled total.
# Called by processes_sample1 before the sampling window.
fn _procs_seed_agnos() {
    _proc_ensure_init();
    _proclist_ensure();
    map_u64_clear(_proc_map);
    var n = sys_proclist(_proclist_buf, PROC_MAX);
    if (n < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (n > PROC_MAX) { n = PROC_MAX; }
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var src = _proclist_buf + i * PROCLIST_REC_SIZE;
        map_u64_set(_proc_map, load64(src + 0), proclist_cpu_ticks(src));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    var pool = _agnos_tick_pool();
    if (pool >= 0) {
        _agnos_pool_prev  = pool;
        _agnos_pool_valid = 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# Fill chakshu's process table from one proclist snapshot.
#
# Writes the shared 56-byte record layout that processes_render and the TUI both
# read, so nothing downstream needs an agnos branch:
#   +0 pid · +8 cpu_pct · +16 rss · +24 state char · +32 comm[16] · +48 uid
# with cpu_pct / rss set to PROC_NA and uid set to 0 (root — agnos is single-user).
#
# Returns the row count, or -1 if this kernel cannot enumerate.
fn _procs_fill_agnos(sort_key) {
    _proc_ensure_init();
    _proclist_ensure();
    _proc_n = 0;

    var n = sys_proclist(_proclist_buf, PROC_MAX);
    if (n < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (n > PROC_MAX) { n = PROC_MAX; }

    # Pooled tick delta for this window. <= 0 disables the whole CPU% column: no
    # baseline yet, an unreadable band, or a window in which the machine took no
    # tick at all (the IF=0 execwait case) all land here, and each must render n/a
    # rather than a division.
    var pool_d = 0;
    var pool = _agnos_tick_pool();
    if (pool >= 0) {
        if (_agnos_pool_valid == 1) {
            if (pool > _agnos_pool_prev) { pool_d = pool - _agnos_pool_prev; }
        }
    }

    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var src = _proclist_buf + i * PROCLIST_REC_SIZE;
        var rec = _proc_recs + _proc_n * PROC_REC_SIZE;

        var pid = load64(src + 0);
        var rssp = proclist_rss_pages(src);

        # CPU%: share of every tick the machine took in this window.
        #  (a) no usable denominator     -> n/a for every row
        #  (b) pid unseen at t0          -> n/a (first sighting; one sample is not a rate)
        #  (c) ticks went BACKWARDS      -> n/a. pid is the slot index in a 16-slot
        #      table and is recycled, and ticks are zeroed at slot ALLOC, so a
        #      decrease means this pid is a different process now — not negative CPU.
        #  (d) nameless row              -> n/a. Kernel threads and each AP's idle
        #      park are charged real ticks (the idle hlt at smp.cyr:467 sits outside
        #      arch_wait()'s halt guard), and only the ELF loader sets a name. Without
        #      this an idle 4-core box shows three phantom busy rows.
        #  (e) clamp to 100 — a tick landing between the two reads can exceed the pool.
        var cpu = PROC_NA;
        if (pool_d > 0) {
            if (load8(src + 24) != 0) {
                var prev = map_u64_get_or(_proc_map, pid, 0 - 1);
                var tks  = proclist_cpu_ticks(src);
                if (prev >= 0) {
                    if (tks >= prev) {
                        cpu = (tks - prev) * 100 / pool_d;
                        if (cpu > 100) { cpu = 100; }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        store64(rec + 0,  pid);
        store64(rec + 8,  cpu);
        # rss is in 4 KiB pages, which is exactly what the shared record and
        # processes_render's `rss * 400 / mem_total_kb` already expect. A slot in the
        # BOOT address space (kmain, the idle task, every kthread) has no user PDEs
        # and legitimately reports 0 — that is a measured zero, not a missing value.
        store64(rec + 16, rssp);
        store64(rec + 24, _proclist_state_char(load64(src + 8)));
        # uid: proclist carries no field, but agnos is SINGLE-USER — its getuid
        # (kernel/core/syscall.cyr:8657) is the literal `return 0`, and no per-task
        # uid exists anywhere in the kernel. So every process IS root; 0 is a KNOWN
        # value, not a missing one, and n/a here would claim ignorance we do not have.
        store64(rec + 48, 0);                                    # uid  — root, always

        # comm: proclist gives 32 bytes, the shared record holds 16. Copy at most
        # 15 and always NUL-terminate — _proc_cmp_comm and strlen both stop at the
        # first null, and an unterminated 16th byte would run into the uid slot.
        var k = 0;
        while (k < 15) {
            var c = load8(src + 24 + k);
            if (c == 0) { break; }
            store8(rec + 32 + k, c);
            k = k + 1;
        }
        store8(rec + 32 + k, 0);

        _proc_n = _proc_n + 1;
        i = i + 1;
    }

    # Sort. CPU and MEM both rank on PROC_NA here, so every row compares equal and
    # the "sorted" table would be arbitrary kernel-slot order wearing a sort's name.
    # Fall back to PID, which is real data. NAME is genuine (proclist carries it) and
    # USER is genuine too — every row is root, and that comparator already tiebreaks
    # on pid, so it degenerates correctly without a special case.
    # Roll the pooled baseline forward so a TUI frame differences against the frame
    # before it rather than against the one-shot seed.
    if (pool >= 0) {
        _agnos_pool_prev  = pool;
        _agnos_pool_valid = 1;
    }
    # Re-seed the per-pid map from THIS snapshot for the next frame.
    map_u64_clear(_proc_map);
    var m = 0;
    while (m < n) {
        var msrc = _proclist_buf + m * PROCLIST_REC_SIZE;
        map_u64_set(_proc_map, load64(msrc + 0), proclist_cpu_ticks(msrc));
        m = m + 1;
    }

    # Sort. MEM and CPU are both real now; CPU only ranks meaningfully when the
    # denominator held, so fall back to PID when the whole column is n/a.
    var key = sort_key;
    if (key == SORT_CPU) {
        if (pool_d <= 0) { key = SORT_PID; }
    }
    _proc_sort(key);
    return _proc_n;
}

#endif
